**4. PROVED / CONDITIONAL / UNVERIFIED — literature audit**

Here \(m\) denotes a prescribed group order, avoiding confusion with the factoring input.

**PROVED — Banks–Shparlinski.** Their *Sato–Tate, cyclicity, and divisibility statistics*, Theorem 12, treats arbitrary isomorphism-invariant subsets of coefficient pairs, for \(1\le A,B\le(p-1)/2\). Corollary 15 gives power-saving transfer from the full finite-field population when
\[
\min(A,B)>p^{1/4+\varepsilon},\qquad AB>p^{1+\varepsilon}.
\]
Their horizontal Theorems 16–18 use
\[
A,B>x^\varepsilon,\quad AB>x^{1+\varepsilon},\quad A,B\le x^{1-\varepsilon};
\]
Theorem 18 concerns divisibility, uniformly for \(m\le(\log x)^K\). These are not marked-point smoothness or short-family hitting theorems. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0609144).

**PROVED — David–Smith upper bound.** Theorem 1 of *Elliptic curves with a given number of points* assumes
\[
A,B\ge\sqrt m\log m,\qquad AB\ge m^{3/2}(\log m)^2,
\]
and bounds the box average of \(M_E(m)\) by \(O(\log\log m/\log m)\). [Paper, including corrigendum](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1108.3539).

**CONDITIONAL — David–Smith asymptotic.** Their Theorem 3, for odd \(m\), assumes short-interval BDH, Conjecture 2, at
\[
\eta=\frac12-\frac{(\gamma+2)\log\log m}{\log m},
\]
and
\[
A,B\ge\sqrt m(\log m)^{1+\gamma}\log\log m,\quad
AB\ge m^{3/2}(\log m)^{2+\gamma}\log\log m.
\]
It gives the corrected \(K(m)m/(\varphi(m)\log m)\) main term. GRH alone does not supply this BDH hypothesis. [Theorem 3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1108.3539).

**PROVED / CONDITIONAL — Martin–Pollack–Smith.** Theorems 1.2–1.4 analyse means and distribution of the arithmetic factor \(K^*(m)\). Proposition 1.6 restates the curve-average asymptotic under BDH for some \(\eta<1/2\), with
\[
A,B>m^{1/2+\varepsilon},\qquad AB>m^{3/2+\varepsilon}.
\]
These results do not establish the proposed covariance. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.0919).

**PROVED — David–Koukoulopoulos–Smith.** Theorem 1.3 gives vertical Sato–Tate, in the automorphism-weighted full population \(\mathcal C_p\), uniformly for
\[
\beta-\alpha\ge p^{-1/2+\varepsilon},
\]
with relative error \(O_{L,\varepsilon}((\log p)^{-L})\). This is a trace-interval theorem, not an arbitrary smoothness-weight theorem or a small coefficient-box theorem. Their general Euler-product machinery requires its own averaging hypotheses. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1510.05935).

**PROVED — Hasse-interval input.** Matomäki–Radziwiłł (2016), Corollary 6, gives the expected density of \(x^{1/u}\)-smooth numbers in almost all intervals of length \(\psi(x)\to\infty\), for fixed \(u\). Corollary 1 gives at least \(\sqrt x/(\log x)^4\) such numbers in every interval of length \(C(u)\sqrt x\); \(C(u)\) need not fit a Hasse interval. [Original paper](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/wp-content/uploads/annals-v183-n3-p06-p.pdf).

**PROVED — prime-centred deduction using the successor theorem.** *Multiplicative functions in short intervals II*, Corollary 1.1, gives power-saving exceptional sets for fixed accuracy, uniformly for bounded real multiplicative functions. Apply it to the smoothness indicator with \(h\asymp\sqrt X\): for every fixed \(u\), all but \(O_u(X^{1-c(u)})\) prime centres have a positive density of \(X^{1/u}\)-smooth integers in a central Hasse subinterval. Corollary 1.3 separately bounds empty smooth intervals by
\[
O_{\varepsilon,\theta}(Xh^{-1/2+\varepsilon}),\quad 2\le h\le X.
\]
An unquantified density-one statement for integer centres alone would not justify restriction to primes. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2007.04290).

**PROVED — smaller smoothness thresholds remain restricted.** Jain, Theorem 1.1, assumes
\[
e^{C(\log X)^{2/3}(\log\log X)^{4/3}}\le y\le X^{1/C},
\quad
h\ge e^{(1+\varepsilon)((11/8)u\log u+4\log\log X)}.
\]
It proves almost-all-interval existence, not optimal ECM-scale smoothness or explicit-family hitting. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2502.10530).

**PROVED / UNVERIFIED — historical ECM distinction.** Pomerance’s weaker almost-all-prime ECM analysis is documented in Hafner–McCurley §3; I could not verify the original Pomerance theorem numbering from the available scan. Lenstra’s optimal \(L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]\) bound is explicitly **Conjecture 2.10**; Proposition 2.8 supplies a rigorous reduction from suitable smooth-order supply. [Hafner–McCurley](https://www.mccurley.org/papers/running.pdf), [Lenstra](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~cs812-1/Lenstra1987.pdf).

**CONDITIONAL — fixed curves.** Barbulescu–Jouve Theorem 1.2(1), proved as Theorem 3.4, gives
\[
\psi_E(x,y)\sim\rho(u)x/\log x
\]
for fixed CM \(E/\mathbb Q\), bounded \(u=\log x/\log y\), under their quadratic-field EH Conjecture 1.1. The non-CM analogue, Theorem 6.2, assumes their stronger elliptic divisibility Hypothesis 1. Their GRH Theorem 6.1 only supplies restricted divisibility averages—among them \(d\le x^{1/5}/(\log x)^4\), supported on primes at most \(x^{1/10}/(\log x)^4\). These concern group orders, without uniform growing-family covariance or marked-point separation. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.11724v2).

**UNVERIFIED — explicit-family conclusion.** I found no useful-\(b\) theorem giving \(J^{-\kappa}\) hitting for the specified explicit prefix \(J\ll p\), unconditionally or from GRH/quadratic-field EH alone. Fixed-level divisibility correlations are accessible to Chebotarev; retaining smoothness and uniformity in growing \(J\) is the missing step. The cited box thresholds demonstrably exclude the proposed family sizes. That establishes non-applicability of these theorems, not impossibility for all known methods.

